<sn><md>### Temperature

Temperature is one of the important factors that affect plant growth.

- Growth is restricted both at very low and very high temperatures.
- The limit of temperature for plant survival is **15–40°C**.
- Optimum temperature for plant growth varies with plant species and varieties.
- Temperature directly affects:
  - Photosynthesis
  - Respiration
  - Transpiration
  - Enzyme activity
  - Protein coagulation
- Photosynthesis, respiration, and transpiration all increase with increasing temperature.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Moisture

- Growth is restricted both at very low and very high levels of soil moisture.
- Growth of many plants is proportional to the amount of moisture present in the growth medium.
- Moisture is required for:
  - Manufacture of carbohydrates
  - Maintenance of hydration of protoplasm
- Internal moisture stress causes reduction in both cell elongation and cell division.
- Roots grow best when soils are well supplied with moisture.
- Too little moisture can result in no plant responses to applied fertilizers.
- Sufficient water is necessary to make the greatest use of fertilizers.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Sunlight

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/assets/sunlight-factor.png)

- The behavior of plants to day length is called **photoperiodism**.
- Some plants flower when the light period is long and the dark period is short.
- Some plants flower when the light period is short and the dark period is long.
- Sunlight is a very important factor for plant growth and flowering.
- For some plants, the light period is critical; for others, the dark period is critical.</md></sn>